00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.529
hi it's Michelle from Lab muffin Beauty
science chemistry PhD and skincare nerd

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:11.910
I've talked about the ins and outs of diy
hand sanitizers quite a bit but

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.809
another question that a lot of people
have started asking me is how do you

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:18.960
look after the skin on your hands when
you're washing and sanitizing them so

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.779
much? *I just want to be pure* how do you
stop your hands from getting so dry and

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:29.220
irritated? so I asked a bunch of
healthcare workers dermatologists

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.149
doctors and nurses including my mum for
their best tips on how to keep your skin

00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:37.200
happy while doing your part to slow down
the spread of the virus and I'll be

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.950
sharing their tips in this video if you
like this sort of content give it a

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.430
thumbs up subscribe to my channel and
click the notification bell so you don't

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:49.920
miss any videos first off why do we care
about keeping our skin in good condition?

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.059
shouldn't we just be focusing on
sanitizing our hands as best we can?

00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:58.680
after my videos on hand sanitizer a lot
of people were asking me why they

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.129
couldn't just use plain alcohol some
people even asked if they could use

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:05.939
plain bleach if they were happy to deal
the consequences on their skin but it's

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:09.930
important to remember that your skin is
your first line of defense against germs

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:14.430
not just the coronavirus but other germs
as well you don't want your skin barrier

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:18.030
to be compromised to the point where
germs can get in easily and cause an

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.159
infection
there's also the fact that cracks and

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:23.970
flakes in your skin are really good
places for germs to hide so you might

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:27.689
not be cleansing your hands properly if
your skin is damaged and of course if

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:31.740
your skin's damaged then cleansers and
sanitizers are going to sting a lot more

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.310
and that means it'll be a lot harder
to rub your hands with soap or sanitizer

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.240
for 20 seconds many times a day which is
what we should be doing to protect

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.619
ourselves properly from coronavirus if
you start getting severe dryness and

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:47.159
cracks in your skin that don't seem to
be improving with the tips here make

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:50.369
sure you talk to a doctor or
dermatologist and they can try to work

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:53.939
out what's happening and fix it but if
you follow these tips hopefully it

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:58.110
doesn't get to that point the first tip
for dealing with hand irritation is by

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:03.600
avoiding it in the first place by using
gentle cleansers and sanitizers almost

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.290
everyone I talked to for this video
mentioned this my mum's actually been a

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:11.550
nurse for 45 years and this was the main
piece of advice she gave for a while she

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.890
had really bad hand dermatitis
where her skin was cracked and raw

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.060
and inflamed even though she was using
tons of hand cream but then the hospital

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:23.200
changed their brand of hand soap and
after a few weeks she didn't actually

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.680
need hand cream at all different people
are sensitive to different ingredients

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.530
so it might be worth trying a few
different products to see which one

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.440
suits you best
my friend who's an anaesthetist mentioned

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.040
that her hospital actually supplies a
few options for people to choose from

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.640
when you're choosing a hand wash there's
a few things that you can look out for

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.120
to improve your chances of finding one
that will work for your skin you've

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.620
probably heard a lot about washing your
hands with soap and water but soap here

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:52.000
actually means any hand cleansing wash
true soaps are alkaline with a high pH

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.890
whereas your skin is low pH and acidic
this means that if you're repeatedly

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.810
using a true soap every day it will
disturb your skin's pH and irritate it

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.560
soap-free cleansers use synthetic detergents
and these generally end up less

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.490
irritating because they can be
formulated at a lower pH they still have

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:13.210
pretty much the same cleansing
properties as a soap they will remove

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.750
the virus from your hands and help it
wash down the drain they can also break

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:21.010
down the lipid envelope around the
coronavirus and inactivate it so choose

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:24.430
a cleanser with a low pH and make sure
it doesn't contain soap in the

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:28.510
ingredients list if you want to reduce
your chances of irritation in general

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:32.050
products with a shorter ingredients list
will give you a lower chance of irritation

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:36.640
than one with a longer list because the
chance of you being sensitive to any of

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.540
the ingredients is lower you might find
that your regular hand wash is actually

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:44.260
too irritating right now because you're
using it so much you can save that for

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.040
later and use something that's more
friendly for sensitive skin while you're

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.550
washing your hands a lot more some of
the very gentle cleansers that some

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:57.040
dermatologists have recommended include
QV soap free wash aquanil and dermal

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.080
500 a few tips for choosing a gentle
hand sanitizer studies have found that

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:07.390
isopropanol which is isopropyl alcohol
or rubbing alcohol is more irritating on

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:11.770
your skin than ethanol so choose a hand
sanitizer with ethanol if you can

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:15.880
sanitizers that contain moisturizing
ingredients like glycerin have also been

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.690
found to reduce irritation and dryness
on your skin different hand sanitizers

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:23.020
also have different ingredients other
than the alcohol and the moisturizing

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.670
ingredients so things like fragrances
and preservatives

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.510
and thickeners so your skin
might tolerate one sanitizer better than

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.060
another
just like with hand wash choosing a hand

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.960
sanitizer with a shorter ingredients
list will probably reduce the chance of

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:39.470
you reacting to a particular
ingredient in it a tip that you might

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.550
not have expected is that hand sanitizer
tends to be less irritating in the long

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:47.300
run than washing your hands if you're in
a position where you have to sanitize

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.540
your hands a lot like if you're
healthcare worker using a properly

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:54.260
formulated hand sanitizer with
moisturizing ingredients is generally

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:57.890
going to be less damaging than
continually washing your hands of course

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:01.010
this depends on the formulas of the
specific hand sanitizers and cleansers

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:04.880
that you're using but generally
sanitizing is better for your hands

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.800
because water is actually irritating
onto a few tips for how to wash your hands

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.210
don't use hot water for washing your
hands hot water strips your skin of its

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.620
natural moisturizing substances much
faster than lukewarm or cold water after

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:23.090
washing pat your hands thoroughly dry
with a towel rather than rubbing it's

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.570
important to get rid of all the water
you can because again water can be

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:30.410
irritating if your hands soak in water
for too long it can break down your skin

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:34.700
and patting rather than rubbing will
reduce irritation wearing gloves in

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:38.900
certain situations can also help it's
also a good idea to avoid getting your

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:43.520
hands wet outside of washing your hands
water is irritating and having your

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.360
hands wet for too long can break down
your skin barrier faster so you should

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.450
be wearing waterproof gloves when you're
washing your dishes dishwashing

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:54.500
detergent is designed to clean greasy
dishes not dirty hands so it's a lot

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:58.520
harsher than hand soap you also use it
for far longer than the 20 seconds you

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:01.970
need for effective hand-washing
waterproof gloves are also a good idea

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:05.780
when you're preparing food some food can
be irritating for your skin and having

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.750
the gloves there to protect your skin
means you won't have to wash your hands

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:12.530
as much when you're wearing gloves make
sure you change them if your hands get

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:16.310
wet inside the glove or too sweaty
because that water can break down your

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:20.180
skin barrier when you're doing housework
or gardening you can wear breathable

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:24.860
fabric gloves to cut down how much you
need to wash your hands it's a good idea

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:29.060
to avoid wearing latex gloves because
latex is a really common allergy if

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:33.170
you're in a place where it's cold and
dry Dr Anjali Mahto recommends wearing a

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:37.230
pair of winter gloves outside cold air
and exposure to wind can make

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:42.600
hand eczema worse using a hand cream is a
pretty obvious solution so here are some

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.220
tips on choosing a hand cream there are
a lot of good hand creams out there if

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.730
you haven't found a good one yet the
dermatologists I talked to recommended

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:55.110
fragrance free hand creams allergies and
irritation are more likely to happen if

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:59.700
your skin is already a bit irritated and
sensitive from all the hand washing so

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.660
avoiding fragrance and really any sort
of additional ingredient is a good

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:07.560
precaution barrier repairing ingredients
to look out for in hand creams are

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.520
glycerin and ceramides
Dr Adeline also recommends niacinamide

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.140
or vitamin b3 for reducing sensitivity
she also recommends hyaluronic acid

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:20.010
which is an effective humectant for
attracting water back into dry skin if

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.790
you're looking for specific product
recommendations a few people recommended

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:27.360
Neutrogena's Norwegian hand formula
there were also recommendations for

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:33.300
Vaseline Aquaphor CeraVe Cetaphil
Vanicream La Roche-Posay's Cicaplast

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:39.600
and Lipikar formulas and Bioderma
Atoderm Dr Aegean Chan also recommended

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.590
3M's Cavilon if you're looking
for a heavy-duty barrier cream it

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:47.310
contains dimethicone and it lasts
through several washes several

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:51.450
dermatologists also recommended using
petrolatum heavy products at night you

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:54.870
can also use these during the day but
they can be a bit sticky and greasy

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:59.430
another possible issue that Dr Adeline
pointed out is that petrolatum has been

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:03.390
known to weaken latex so if you're a
health care worker it's a good idea not

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:08.570
to use these creams before wearing latex
gloves a few tips on using hand creams

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:12.780
remember to apply the hand cream all
over your hands including between your

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:17.010
fingers try to use hand creams more
frequently not just that night but

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:20.730
regularly throughout the day it's a good
idea to keep a few tubes of hand cream

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:24.630
in different places so you don't forget
to use it so for example on your bedside

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:29.550
table next to the TV on your desk in
your car you can make a few sets of hand

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.700
cream with small bottles of hand
sanitizer if you have it so you can keep

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:36.390
a set in your car a set in your handbag
and a set at home so you don't forget

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:40.260
to take with you when you leave the
house for a deep overnight moisturizing

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:44.400
treatment you can cover your hands with
hand cream then put on cotton gloves the

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:47.880
gloves stop the hand cream from coming
off onto your sheets so that your skin

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.769
can soak it in more effectively if you
don't have cotton gloves

00:08:50.769 --> 00:08:54.579
you can use socks Dr Papri also
recommends soaking your hands for 10

00:08:54.579 --> 00:08:58.420
minutes in tepid water then pat your
hands dry and then put on the hand cream

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:02.559
while your hands are still damp before
you put on the gloves if you have cracks

00:09:02.559 --> 00:09:06.699
in your hands a few dermatologists
recommend sealing the cracks up with

00:09:06.699 --> 00:09:10.689
either superglue or liquid bandaid this
protects your skin and makes it a lot

00:09:10.689 --> 00:09:15.009
less painful while your skin heals if
your skin does get dry and damaged and

00:09:15.009 --> 00:09:19.029
flaky resist the urge to pick at your
skin this can make your skin feel worse

00:09:19.029 --> 00:09:23.470
and open it up to infections
if you develop blisters Dr Joyce also

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:26.980
recommends not popping them because the
same thing can happen do you have any

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:30.369
other tips for looking after your hands?
share them with us in the comments I

